WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
Connection between PFSS and Process Yield

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:08.600
Welcome back to our session on Processing for Six Sigma.

3
00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:15.200
Recall, that we looked at a little process consisting of three steps, three operations.

4
00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:22.233
Operation 1, 2, 3. Also recall, that each of these operations

5
00:00:22.233 --> 00:00:25.199
created a deliverable.

6
00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:31.600
That the input coming into operation 1 adds value to what came in

7
00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:35.433
and puts it out with higher value.

8
00:00:35.433 --> 00:00:40.566
So, from a graphical point of view. If this is the scale of value,

9
00:00:40.566 --> 00:00:46.866
our value steadily increases as we move through the process.

10
00:00:46.866 --> 00:00:50.866
Okay. Now, here's something interesting.

11
00:00:50.866 --> 00:00:57.666
We said that each operation has certain X’s.

12
00:00:57.666 --> 00:01:03.999
X1, X2, through XN.

13
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:09.266
And that these X’s if you will, are what we call

14
00:01:09.266 --> 00:01:14.832
critical to process variables. These are my CTP’s.

15
00:01:14.833 --> 00:01:18.066
The ones that are among the vital few

16
00:01:18.066 --> 00:01:22.266
are critical to the quality of the deliverable.

17
00:01:22.266 --> 00:01:27.299
The trivial many down here. The X’s that are among the trivial many, are not CTP’s

18
00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:30.666
because they're not critical to process operation.

19
00:01:30.666 --> 00:01:37.666
They may be essential to process operation. But they're not critical to process operation.

20
00:01:37.666 --> 00:01:42.666
In other words, how much light in the room

21
00:01:42.666 --> 00:01:46.266
has little to do, but it is essential to see your work.

22
00:01:46.266 --> 00:01:50.832
The process might be able to operate in the dark. See, it’s is not critical to process.

23
00:01:50.833 --> 00:01:53.833
That's important to understand.

24
00:01:53.833 --> 00:01:59.033
That the vital few X’s

25
00:01:59.033 --> 00:02:02.166
are the critical to process variables.

26
00:02:02.166 --> 00:02:07.932
Now, we have to also recognize that every one of these

27
00:02:07.933 --> 00:02:14.933
critical to process variables has on its scale of measure.

28
00:02:14.933 --> 00:02:21.399
A standard, like an upper spec limit and a lower spec limit

29
00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:25.400
and a target value that's optimal.

30
00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:31.966
In other words, this target value right here, is the optimal setting

31
00:02:31.966 --> 00:02:35.966
for that vital few variable, for that critical to process variable.

32
00:02:35.966 --> 00:02:42.299
Now, if all of the critical to process variables are at their optimal setting,

33
00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:48.433
then the yield from this operation will be high.

34
00:02:48.433 --> 00:02:55.433
So, there is a direct correlation to the goodness of the settings of the vital few variables.

35
00:02:55.433 --> 00:03:01.633
The critical to process variables and the operations metric called yield.

36
00:03:01.633 --> 00:03:08.633
Now, if this yield. And, I'm going to blow this up a little bit.

37
00:03:08.633 --> 00:03:15.566
To where we've got operation 1, flowing into operation 2.

38
00:03:15.566 --> 00:03:21.599
You see, if this yield right here and this yield.

39
00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:26.766
Let's say that this is 90% and that that's 80%,

40
00:03:26.766 --> 00:03:32.332
then the throughput yield, or the probability of going for both operations defect free

41
00:03:32.333 --> 00:03:38.233
is .90 times .80 or .72 or 72%.

42
00:03:38.233 --> 00:03:42.499
That’s to say there's a 72% probably of going through here defect free.

43
00:03:42.500 --> 00:03:46.800
Now, listen to this. I’m going to give it to you real quick. This is important.

44
00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:53.766
If I find the vital few, the critical to process variables, at operation 1

45
00:03:53.766 --> 00:03:59.632
and I find their optimal setting. Their most advantageous setting.

46
00:03:59.633 --> 00:04:04.199
And I set those variables there. Then this yield is going to increase.

47
00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:10.366
This yield is going to go from like 90% to maybe 99.

48
00:04:10.366 --> 00:04:16.366
If I do it at this operation as well. The yield might go from 80% to 90.

49
00:04:16.366 --> 00:04:23.332
So, the net effect is .99 times .90 is approximately .90.

50
00:04:23.333 --> 00:04:30.166
Compared to 72, which mean there's been about an 18% improvement in yield

51
00:04:30.166 --> 00:04:33.166
from this operation.

52
00:04:33.166 --> 00:04:39.099
Now, as that yield improves from that process.

53
00:04:39.100 --> 00:04:43.100
See, there's that process right there, three operations.

54
00:04:43.100 --> 00:04:50.100
Process A and I have process B, and I have process C that come together,

55
00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:54.400
to blend into yet another process and that yield goes up.

56
00:04:54.400 --> 00:05:01.266
Then the yield of the whole factory goes up. Which means the profits go up.

57
00:05:01.266 --> 00:05:07.499
And that's the connectivity that we’re looking for and how it relates back

58
00:05:07.500 --> 00:05:10.966
to process capability.

59
00:05:10.966 --> 00:05:15.966
Thank you for choosing SSMI

